<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="salicylate biosynthesis II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: CPD-110 &quot;Salicylate&quot;| is an aromatic compound that is is common throughout the plant kingdom and is also found in some bacteria. Salicylate is an important phytohormone, mediating both local and systemic defense responses in plants. It induces flowering and is involved in resistance against pathogen infection through the systematic acquired resistance mechanism |CITS: [12873532][15283665]|. Two pathways have been described for the biosynthesis of |FRAME: CPD-110| in plants. In Arabidopsis, the major part of |FRAME: CPD-110| is derived from |FRAME: ISOCHORISMATE|, as is the case in bacteria. However, whereas bacteria employ an |FRAME: EC-4.2.99.21| to catalyze the turnover of isochorismate to pyruvate and salicylate (see |FRAME: PWY-6406|), plants do not contain an ortholog of that enzyme. Instead, plants utilize the product of the |FRAME: AT5G13320| gene, which catalyzes ATP- and Mg&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;-dependent conjugation of |FRAME: ISOCHORISMATE| with |FRAME: GLT|. The product, |FRAME: CPD-24912|, is unstable, and could decompose spontaneously to form |FRAME: CPD-110|. However, this decomposition was found to be catalyzed by the product of the |FRAME: AT5G67160| gene. Together, |FRAME: AT5G13320| and |FRAME: AT5G67160| form a two-step metabolic pathway to produce |FRAME: CPD-110| from |FRAME: ISOCHORISMATE| |CITS: [31760159]|. The minor plant pathway for |FRAME: CPD-110|, which is derived from |FRAME: PHE|, is described in |FRAME: PWY-981|.</Description>
