    <DataNode elementId="AT5G13320-MONOMER" textLabel="benzoyl-amino acid synthetase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9LYU4" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="600" centerY="535" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup, THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE, dreher">The PBS3/GH3.12 enzyme is a member of the acyl-adenylate/thioester-forming enzyme family, often referred to as the firefly luciferase family |CITS: [12084835][19189963]|. More specifically, it belongs to a subfamily that catalyzes reactions without forming a thioester intermediate |CITS: [19189963]|. This enzyme has been linked to salicyclic acid accumulation and plant defense responses on the basis of &lt;i&gt;pbs3&lt;/i&gt; mutant phenotypes |CITS:[10224270][17468220][19189963]|. Although PBS3 does not act on salicyclic acid (or other phytohormones tested), &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; adenylation assays reveal that this enzyme can accept a large number of acyl substrates including benzoate, 4-amino-3-hydroxybenzoate, &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-cinnamate, and others (see |CITS: [19189963]| for details). It shows a preference for compounds with an aromatic ring that have 4-(para-) substitutions. PBS3 can conjugate amino acids to several of these substrates &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; with varying levels of specificity. 4-hydroxybenzoic acid (4-HBA) can be conjugated to a number of different amino acids, whereas 4-aminobenzoic acid (pABA) is only conjugated to glutamate |CITS:[19189963]|. The &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; substrate(s) for PBS3 remain to be determined, but 4HBA and pABA are the most likely candidates. 4HBA, normally produced at low levels by all plants, is related to a number of biochemical pathways and processes including ubiquinone biosynthesis, specialized metabolite biosynthesis, cell wall metabolism, and plant defense responses. Meanwhile, pABA-Glu, a predicted breakdown product of folate catabolism |CITS:[16623903]| may also be involved in regulating plant defense by sequestering free pABA away from pathogens |CITS:[19189963]|. The relationship between PBS3/GH3.12 and salicylate production and signaling in plants requires further investigation. However, salicylic acid appears to negatively regulate the activity of this enzyme, most likely as a competitive inhibitor. In an &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assay using pABA, the IC&lt;sub&gt;50&lt;/sub&gt; for salicylate (SA) is 15 &amp;mu;M |CITS: [19189963]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G13320-MONOMER" />
